\section{Unitary Completion vs Unitary Dilation}\label{sec: discussion}

One might wonder how our approach based on unitary completion compares to the unitary dilation technique. We will compare and contrast the two related techniques in this section. 

The unitary dilation of a matrix is given by~\Cref{def:dilation}. Note that the dilation operator, $\Dm_{\Am}$, is well defined as $\In-\Am^{*}\Am\geq 0$ is a positive semi-definite matrix under the contraction assumption. 

\begin{definition}
Let $\Am$ be contraction, i.e., $\|\Am\|_2\leq 1$, then the unitary dilation $\Um_{\Am}$ of $\Am$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\Tilde{\Um}_{\Am}=\left(
            \begin{array}{cc}
              \Am & \Dm_{\Am^{*}} \\
              \Dm_{\Am} & -\Am^{*} \\
            \end{array}
          \right), \label{eq:ud}
\end{equation}
where, $\Dm_{\Am}=\sqrt{\In-\Am^{*}\Am}$. 
\label{def:dilation}
\end{definition}

As per the Sz.-Nagy dilation theorem, every contraction on a Hilbert space has a unitary dilation which is unique up to an unitary equivalence \cite{schaffer1955unitary}. In contrast, the concept of unitary completion only makes sense when the non-zero columns of $\Am$ are unitary according to~\Cref{def: completion}. Thus, unitary completion is not applicable to every contraction on the Hilbert space.  

Both dilation and completion exist for the sigma basis LCNU terms, $\Am_l$. Let us focus on understanding this relation for the remainder of the section. First, note that the dilation of $\Am_l$, $\tilde{\Um}_l \coloneqq \tilde{\Um}_{\Am_l}$, on an ancillary system of the form $\ket{0} \ket{\psi}$ gives,
\begin{equation*}
\tilde{\Um}_{l}|0\ra|\psi\ra=|0\ra \Am_l  |\psi\ra+|1\ra \Dm_{\Am_l} |\psi\ra,
\end{equation*}
similar to the expression in \Cref{eq: Umact}.
The term $(\In-\Am^*_l\Am_l)^2$ can be simplified as $\In-\Am^T_l\Am_l$ using properties of the sigma basis given in~\cite{gnanasekaran2024efficient}. Thus, we can simplify $\tilde{\Um}_l$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq: Um_dil}
\tilde{\Um}_{l}=\begin{bmatrix}
	\Am_l &\In-\Am_l\Am_l^T	\\
              \In-\Am^{T}_l\Am_l  & -\Am^{T}_l  \\
            \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Note that $\tilde{\Um}_{l}$ is unitary even without the negative sign in the (2,2) block position and can be shown using the properties of the sigma basis. To simplify analysis, let us ignore the negative sign as it is unitarily equivalent (up to a controlled $\sigma_z$ gate). With this, $\tilde{\Um}_{l}$ is also a permutation matrix and can be expressed as a sequence of Toffoli gates. 



~\Cref{thm: Udil} shows that in general $\Tilde{\Um}_l$ requires more $C^n X$ gates than $\Um_l$ and hence, is less efficient compared to our unitary completion approach.  Note that while there is a reduction in the number of single qubit gates, the bottleneck in circuit complexities arise from the  $C^n X$ gate. With our completion based approach, we \textit{always} require a single $C^n X$. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: Udil}
    $\Tilde{\Um}_l$ as defined in~\Cref{eq: Um_dil} can be implemented using a single qubit gate and $2s+1$ $C^m X$ gates where $s = \sum_{i} \left[ \sigma_i \in \{ \Sp, \Sm \} \right]$ and $m \leq n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We can write $\Tilde{\Um}_l$ as,
\begin{align*}
    \Tilde{\Um}_l &= \Tilde{\Um}_{l,1}\Tilde{\Um}_{l,2} \\
    &= \begin{bmatrix}
	\In-\Am_l\Am_l^T & \Am_l	\\
             \Am^{T}_l & \In-\Am^{T}_l\Am_l    \\
            \end{bmatrix}  \begin{bmatrix}
                 & \In \\
                 \In & 
            \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
We can construct $\Tilde{\Um}_{l,2}$ simply by applying $X$ gate on the ancilla qubit and $\Id$ on the other qubits. Let's focus on constructing $\Tilde{\Um}_{l,1}$.

 Without loss of generality, consider \[\Am_l = \sigma_1 \otimes \dots \otimes \sigma_s \otimes \sigma_{s+1} \otimes \dots \otimes \sigma_n\] 
where, 
\begin{align*}
   \sigma_1, \dots \sigma_s \in S_1 &= \{ \Sp, \Sm\} \\
   \sigma_{s+1}, \dots, \sigma_{n} \in S_2 &= \{ \Sp\Sm, \Sm\Sp,\Id \}
\end{align*}
 Then $\Am_l^T = \sigma_1^T \otimes \dots \otimes \sigma_s^T \otimes \sigma_{s+1} \otimes \dots \otimes \sigma_n$. $\Am_l$ is a binary matrix by construction. Suppose, $\Am_l(r,c) = 1$. Using~\Cref{lem: Arow}, 
\[
\Am_l(r,c)=1 \iff \sigma_p(q_r(p), q_c(p)) = 1\quad \forall p,
\]
where, $r = \sum_{p=1}^{n} 2^{n-p} q_r(p)$, $c = \sum_{p=1}^{n} 2^{n-p} q_c(p)$ and $q_r, q_c \in \{0,1\}$. By definition, 
\begin{align*}
    \sigma_i \in S_1 &\implies q_r(i) \neq q_c(i) \\
    \sigma_i \in S_2 &\implies q_r(i) = q_c(i). 
\end{align*} 
Then by construction, the binary representation of $r$ and $c$ differ in $s$ bits. 

We have,
\begin{align*}
\Am_l(r,c) = 1 &\implies
    \Am_l^T(c,r)=1, \\
    &\implies \tilde{\Um}_{l,1}(r,c+2^n) =1 \\
    &\implies \tilde{\Um}_{l,1}(c+2^n,r) =1 
\end{align*}
Then, we can construct $\Um_l$ by permuting rows $r$ and $c + 2^n $ of the Identity matrix. As the binary representation of the two rows differ by $s+1$ bits, we require $2s+1$ $C^n X$ gates to permute them using~\Cref{cor:kbits_swap}. 

If there are $2^k$ non-zero rows in $\Am_l$ (i.e., $k$ factors in the tensor product are $\Id$), the same arguments used in the proof of~\Cref{thm: Acomp} extend here to conclude that we would need $2s+1$ $C^{n-k} X$ gates with $n-k$ control qubits and one target qubit.  
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Note that when $s=0$ in~\Cref{thm: Udil},  $\tilde{\Um}_l = \Um_l$, i.e., the unitary produced by dilation is essentially the same (ignoring the phase factor) as the one produced by our completion technique. 
\end{remark}

\section{Conclusions}\label{sec: conc}
We presented a novel approach for efficient LCNU decomposition for structured sparse matrices using the sigma basis, where the number of terms scale poly-logarithmically with the matrix size. Given that this basis is comprised of non-unitary operators, we employed the concept of unitary completion and presented a simple procedure for construction of efficient quantum circuits for implementing this completion. We showed that such circuits only have slightly increased overhead in comparison to the circuits associated with Pauli basis LCU decomposition. We further incorporated the unitary completion circuits within Hadamard-like test circuits for the evaluation of observables that commonly arise in VQAs. We also developed an approach for block encoding of an arbitrary operator given its decomposition in the sigma basis, which could be used in a variety of fully fault-tolerant algorithms. We established the resources needed for such a block encoding based on the sigma basis decomposition showing an exponentially smaller number of gates and circuit depth at the expense of a slight increase in the number of ancilla qubits needed as compared to the Pauli basis-based block encoding. We illustrated our approach by explicitly computing the sigma basis decomposition of matrices arising in several PDE examples and showed an exponential reduction in the number of decomposition terms in comparison to the Pauli basis. We also presented efficient numerical and semi-analytical approaches for computing the sigma basis decomposition for an arbitrary matrix. 

In future, it would be worthwhile to explore the use of sigma basis decomposition in other domains beyond quantum linear algebra applications. It would also be fruitful to further formalize the concept of linear combination of ``things" whereby one can develop tailored sigma basis type sets which exploit particular structure of the problem at hand to gain efficiency.   

