\section{Two special classes of $n$-enclosing circles}\label{sec:SpecialClass}

\subsection{First special class}\label{subsec:1stClass}
For any positive integer $k$, we may define the unique circle $\calS_k$
that goes through the following points: $A_1(-k,0),A_2(-k,1), A_3(0,k+1),
A_4(1,k+1), A_5(k+1,1), A_6(k+1,0), A_7(1,-k), A_8(0,-k).$ Suppose
$\calS_k$ encloses exactly $f(k)$ lattice points, then $\calS_k$ is a possible candidate for the largest
$f(k)$-enclosing circle. If $\calS_k$ is indeed the largest $f(k)$-enclosing circle then $f(k)$ is MC and
$$R_{f(k)}=\sqrt{k^2+k+\frac12}.$$
We have already seen this is true when $k=1$ and $k=2$ in the case $n=4$ in Theorem \ref{thm:R0-4}. We depicted these circles (and many others) in Figure \ref{fig:impactingFactorAll} on page \pageref{fig:impactingFactorAll} for $k\le 18$, $k\ne 4,7,12, 16$.

In general, it is not hard to see that
\begin{equation*}
    f(k)=4\sum_{j=1}^k \left(\left\lceil \sqrt{k^2 + k +  \frac12 - \Big(j - \frac12\Big)^2} +  \frac12 \right\rceil - 1 \right) \sim \pi  R_{f(k)}^2=\pi\left(k^2+k+\frac12\right).
\end{equation*}
