\section*{Viscoelastic model of condensate growth}\label{sec:Model}

To construct our model, we assume that condensate material can exist in two distinct forms: a precursor form, and an assembly-competent (or scaffold) form.
Condensate material can be converted between these two configurations by active chemical reactions.
To ensure controllable condensate formation at specific locations in space, active cores drive the localised conversion of precursor material into scaffold material, which then rapidly self-assembles into a compact, condensed state.
%Active cores drive the nucleation of condensates at a specific location in space by converting condensate material from a precursor form into an assembly-competent (or scaffold) form that rapidly self-assembles into a compact, condensed state. 
Further condensate growth results from the incorporation of new material via additional conversion reactions in the condensate interior.
%Further condensate growth can then be accelerated by the preferential partitioning of enzymes into the condensate, resulting in further reactions within the condensate bulk.
%Internally maintained droplets? Focus on interior? Embedded in cell?
%These reactions are localised to the droplet interior or ... we are only interested in the interior.
We describe the boundary between the condensate interior and the surrounding environment as a sharp interface across which precursor material can be exchanged.
For a porous gel-like material, this interface is defined as the edge of the connected scaffold network.
For a droplet-like phase-separated material, the interface emerges naturally from phase separation. % and is given by
%can be defined as the spatial extent within which the volume fraction of scaffold material is larger than some threshold value. 

To describe the distribution of precursor~($P$) and scaffold~($S$) components within a condensate, we take a continuum approach in which the relative volume fractions of $P$ and $S$ are given by $\phi_{P}(\vect{r},t)$ and $\phi_{S}(\vect{r},t)$, respectively.
%We also assume that the condensate is embedded within a closed system, such as a cell. %Below we do this...
Below, we derive a model that describes how $\phi_{P}$ and $\phi_{S}$ evolve within a condensate over time. %due to... %, enabling predictions for condensate size, density, and stress state, as functions of time.
