\section{Larger Cylinders}\label{sec:Larger_cylinder}

In contrast to the thin-cylinder limit, dimer coverings on larger cylinders cannot be embedded in a plane, naively necessitating a three-dimensional arrangement in experiments. Nonetheless, as we argue in Sec.~\ref{sec:StateCreation}, if we can move the sites around during the state creation process (or perform gates on qubits which are sufficiently far apart), we can construct these resonating dimer states through dynamical planar geometries.

Here we generalize the constructions of Sec.~\ref{sec:thin} by breaking our cylinder into annular strips, which are analogous to the unit cells of the eye or hourglass models. For a cylinder of arbitrary width, we find that there exists a systematic method to describe the dimer coverings, and show that the Rokhsar-Kivelson state can accordingly be written as a matrix product state. Some details are relegated to Appendix~\ref{sec:proof_of_modules}.
%, we provide a detailed proof that these annular strips satisfy the necessary conditions to support valid dimer coverings, along with the explicit construction method. In the main text, we will directly present the naming convention and the conditions that must be satisfied.


