\section{Construction of dimer coverings on YC-$2N$ strips}\label{sec:proof_of_modules}





In Sec.~\ref{sec:Larger_cylinder}, we labeled the dimer configurations on a YC-$2N$ strip by specifying $L,R,u$.  The sequence of binary digits $L=(l_1,l_2,\cdots l_N)$ label the left pointing triangles.  If there is a dimer touching $j'th$ triangle point, then $l_j=1$, otherwise $l_j=0$.  The digits composing $R=(r_1,r_2,\cdots r_N)$ similarly label the right pointing triangle,   $u=1$ or 0 depending on  if there is a dimer that touches that point from below.  See Fig.~\ref{fig:module_conn_1} for several examples.  Here we show how to construct a dimer covering from these labels, demonstrating that they uniquely specify the state.  While we focus on YC-$2N$ strips here, the same construction also works for the XC-$2N$ strips.

We begin by showing that for a consistent dimer covering, 
