As an intermediate step we will have a situation where the left-half of the system is in the resonating dimer state (shown in light pink in Fig.~\ref{fig:eyemodel}), while all of the atoms on the the right half are in the ground state (shown in white). We want to manipulate the two atoms in the dark red area, (3,5) and (3,6),  to grow the resonating dimer state. Initially the (3,1) and (3,2) atoms  are in a Bell state, and the wavefunction of the whole system can be represented as
\begin{align}
|\psi\rangle&=\cdots\eyeB\eyeA\eyeBl\eye\eye\cdots
\end{align}
Here we denote $\eyeBl=\frac{\eyeBlu+\eyeBld}{\sqrt{2}}$. 



We engineer a situation where $\Omega_\alpha=0$ unless $\alpha=(3,5)$ or $(3,6)$.  We will then specify $\Omega_{3,5}(t)=\Omega_{3,6}(t)=\Omega(t)$ and $\Delta_{3,5}(t)=\Delta_{3,6}(t)=\Delta(t)$.  Since $\Omega=0$ on all other sites, the values of $\Delta$ on them are irrelevant.  Under these conditions all parts of the wavefunction are frozen except for the degrees of freedom in plaquette n=3.  This accessible Hilbert space is spanned by the 2 states
$|1\rangle=(|\eyeBlu\rangle+|\eyeBld\rangle)/\sqrt{2},|2\rangle=(|\eyeBa\rangle+|\eyeBb\rangle)/\sqrt{2}$.  Projecting the Hamiltonian into this space yields
\begin{align}
H&=\frac{\Omega(t)}{2}(|2\rangle\langle1|+|1\rangle\langle2|)-\Delta(t) |2\rangle\langle 2|
%( |\eyeBa\rangle\langle \eyeBlu|+
%|\eyeBb\rangle\langle \eyeBld| + H.C.) \\
%&+\Delta(t) (|\eyeBa\rangle\langle \eyeBa|+|\eyeBb\rangle\langle \eyeBb|)\nonumber
\end{align}
We can adiabatically evolve the state from $|\eyeBlu\rangle+|\eyeBld\rangle$ to $|\eyeBa\rangle+|\eyeBb\rangle$ by sweeping $\Delta$ from a large negative value to a large positive value, keeping $\Omega$ a finite constant.  The minimal gap during this procedure is $\Omega$ (should be proportional to $\Omega$, actually $E_{gap} = \sqrt{\Delta^2+\Omega^2}$) which sets the maximum speed for the adiabatic sweep.  


