\begin{rem}\label{r:18-resolutions}
    For each (2, 2)-partition of $\{ 1, 2, 3, 4\}$, there are $4+4-1=7$ choices of resolution satisfying \eqref{n=4-cond1},\eqref{n=4-cond2}. Each pair of partitions has exactly one of these in common, which is not in common with the other partition. So in total, Theorem \ref{thm:four-point} shows that $3\cdot 7 - 3 = 18$ of the $64$ locally projective crepant resolutions of $\cM_\theta(Q,\alpha)$ define nonprojective crepant resolutions of  $\cM_0(Q,\alpha)$. The remaining 46 are projective over $\cM_0(Q,\alpha)$, as we verified using a computer.
