%, we must have more than two vertices, since for two vertices, if $\theta$ is nonzero, it is generic, and the singular locus of $\cM_\theta(Q,\alpha)$ has a unique minimal stratum which corresponds to the decomposition $\alpha = c \alpha'$ for $c=\gcd(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$ the maximum positive integer dividing both entries of $\alpha$, and $\alpha':=\alpha/c$. As explained in \cite{BS21}, the singularity here does not admit a projective crepant resolution unless it is either already smooth ($c=1$ or $\cM_{\theta}(Q,\alpha)$ is a point), $\cM_\theta(Q,\alpha')$ a surface (so the singularity is of the form $\text{Sym}^c(\bC^2)$), or $c=2$ and $\dim \cM_\theta(Q,\alpha') = 4$, the O'Grady case, where a projective symplectic resolution is unique and given by taking the blow-up of the singular locus.) 


