\begin{abstract}
We present the design and implementation of a new lifetime-aware tensor offloading framework for GPU memory expansion using low-cost PCIe-based solid-state drives (SSDs). Our framework, \name{}, is developed explicitly for large language model (LLM) training with multiple GPUs and multiple SSDs. Its design is driven by our observation that the active tensors take only a small fraction {(1.7\% on average)} of allocated GPU memory in each LLM training iteration, the inactive tensors are usually large and will not be used for a long period of time, creating ample opportunities for offloading/prefetching tensors to/from slow SSDs without stalling the GPU training process. \name{} accurately estimates the lifetime (active period of time in GPU memory) of each tensor with the profiling of the first few iterations in the training process. With the tensor lifetime analysis, \name{} will generate an optimized tensor offloading/prefetching plan and integrate it into the compiled LLM program via PyTorch. \name{} has a runtime tensor migration engine to execute the offloading/prefetching plan via GPUDirect storage, which allows direct tensor migration between GPUs and SSDs for alleviating the CPU bottleneck and maximizing the SSD bandwidth utilization. In comparison with state-of-the-art studies such as ZeRO-Offload and ZeRO-Infinity, we show that \name{} improves the training performance of various LLMs by {1.47}$\times$ on average, and achieves {80.7}\% of the ideal performance assuming unlimited GPU memory. 
%\name{} consists of three major components: (i) a lightweight tensor lifetime profiler that can accurately estimate the lifetime of each tensor based on the execution graph generated by PyTorch; (2) a lifetime-aware tensor offloading plan generator that can 


 %   As the memory demand of large language models (LLMs) is increasing much faster than GPU memory capacity, the well-known GPU memory wall is exacerbated. Scaling the capacity of the high-bandwidth memory (HBM) in GPUs is both technically challenging and prohibitively expensive. In contrast, flash-based SSDs offer larger capacity at a lower cost. In this project, we aim to build a cost-effective multi-GPU LLM training system that can expand GPU memory with low-cost SSDs while minimizing the negative impact on the training performance.

%    Through comprehensive characterization of multi-GPU LLM training, we demonstrate that aggregating four SSDs per GPU (16GB/s bandwidth) can achieve near-ideal performance. Our analysis reveals two critical insights: only a small fraction (<5\%) of tensors are active during training, and many large tensors remain inactive for long periods—creating significant opportunities for efficient GPU-SSD tensor migration. We present a complete tensor migration framework that includes: (1) a tensor lifetime profiler that extracts detailed tensor semantic information, (2) a migration plan generator that selects optimal tensors for offloading based on GPU memory usage and tensor inactive time, and (3) a runtime migration engine using GPUDirect Storage with synchronization barriers to address timing uncertainties, along with a custom GPU memory manager to mitigate memory fragmentation. Our framework integrates seamlessly with PyTorch, enabling developers to efficiently train LLMs substantially larger than GPU memory capacity while minimizing performance impact and significantly reducing infrastructure costs.

\end{abstract}

