\paragraph{Comparison with Gaussian VAE}

To better understand the behavior of our proposed spCauchy latent distribution, we compare it directly to the standard Gaussian VAE baseline on MNIST. Both models use the same convolutional architecture and training setup as in the previous section, differing only in the choice of latent distribution. For fairness, we estimate the minimum reconstruction error over the test set across 40 epochs as our primary metric. Since the KL divergence plays a structurally different role in both models, being a regularizer towards a standard normal prior in the Gaussian case and towards a uniform distribution on the sphere in the spCauchy case, we focus our comparison on the reconstruction loss, which provides a more direct and fair measure of representational quality.

We evaluated both models across a range of latent dimensions, comparing Euclidean latent spaces \(\mathbb{R}^d\) with spherical latent spaces \(\mathbb{S}^d\) for \(d \in \{2, 3, 5, 10, 20\}\).  For higher latent dimensions (\(d \geq 5\)), we observed that both models struggled with the original learning rate. Therefore, we reduced the learning rate from 0.0005 to 0.0001 in these settings to ensure stable training.

Importantly, while the Gaussian model benefits from having a prior from the same distributional family (standard normal), the spCauchy model must always deviate significantly from its uniform prior to encode any information. Despite this disadvantage, the spherical Cauchy model consistently outperforms the Gaussian baseline in terms of reconstruction accuracy.

