As shown in Table~\ref{tab:mnist-comparison}, the spCauchy-VAE achieves lower reconstruction losses across all tested dimensions. The differences are modest but consistent, demonstrating the robustness of the spCauchy latent space even in low-dimensional regimes.

It is important to note that this comparison is inherently biased in favor of the Gaussian model in higher dimensions. A Gaussian latent space in \(\mathbb{R}^d\) requires \(2d\) parameters (mean and variance), while the spCauchy model only uses \(d + 2\) parameters (a unit vector \(\mu \in \mathbb{S}^d\) and a scalar \(\rho\)). This means that for \(d > 2\), the Gaussian latent space has strictly more representational capacity. Despite this, the spCauchy-VAE achieves better performance using fewer parameters and with lower computational complexity.

If we were to compare the models at equal latent ``parameter budget'', that is, selecting the hypersphere dimension such that the total number of latent parameters matches that of the Gaussian VAE, the advantage of the spCauchy-VAE would be even more pronounced. In such a setup, the spherical model would operate in much higher dimensions than the Gaussian, likely leading to substantially stronger performance and an even greater gap in reconstruction quality.

These results suggest that the spCauchy distribution not only provides a geometrically consistent latent space but also delivers competitive and often superior empirical performance-despite its simplicity, fewer parameters, and the challenge of deviating from a uniform prior.

We note that we did not include a direct comparison with the hyperspherical VAE based on the von Mises-Fisher (vMF) distribution introduced by \citet{davidson2018hyperspherical}, as we were unable to find a complete and numerically stable implementation of the KL divergence derived in the appendix of their paper. The official implementation relies on a discrete approximation of the KL term using \texttt{torch.nn.functional.kl\_div}, which makes it incompatible with our closed-form evaluation of the KL divergence. Nevertheless, we expect that the vMF-VAE would perform comparably in terms of reconstruction quality, as it also benefits from encoding on the hypersphere.

However, the vMF approach comes with significantly higher computational cost. It requires evaluation of ratios of modified Bessel functions, often suffers from numerical instability, and relies on rejection sampling to implement the reparameterization trick. These steps introduce additional complexity and slow down convergence, particularly in higher dimensions where the normalization constant becomes increasingly problematic. In contrast, the spCauchy distribution has a closed-form density, supports efficient and fully differentiable sampling via Möbius transformations, and allows for stable approximation of the KL divergence.

Moreover, the heavy-tailed nature of the spCauchy distribution provides additional robustness, particularly in high-dimensional settings, where over-regularization and posterior collapse become more likely. This parallels prior observations where heavy-tailed priors, such as the Student-\(t\) distribution, have been shown to outperform Gaussian priors by maintaining latent flexibility. For these reasons, we consider the spCauchy-VAE a practical and computationally efficient alternative to the vMF-VAE for modeling hyperspherical latent spaces.

\subsection{Generative Molecular Discovery and Latent Space Characterization for SMILES using a High-Dimensional Spherical Cauchy VAE}

Building on our initial validation of the Spherical Cauchy VAE (SpCauchy-VAE) on the MNIST dataset, we now extend the framework to a substantially more complex and high-dimensional domain: the generative modeling of chemical molecules. This section evaluates the scalability of the SpCauchy-VAE and its ability to learn meaningful representations on the hypersphere \( S^{d-1} \), with a focus on larger dimension, more specifically a latent space of dimensionality \( d=128 \). We aim both to demonstrate the model’s ability to capture the sequential and structured nature of molecular data and to investigate the potential of spherical latent geometry for controlled molecular generation.

We represent molecular structures using the Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry System (SMILES)~\citep{Weininger1988SMILES}, a compact and machine-readable string format encoding atomic types, bond types, ring structures, and branches. Its widespread use in cheminformatics, along with its discrete and compositional structure, makes it an ideal testbed for evaluating the generative capacity of high-dimensional latent VAEs. The latent space in our setting is constrained to the unit hypersphere, encouraging representations to reside on \( S^{127} \) and allowing the model to focus on directional features rather than magnitude. This yields a natural interpretation of molecular similarity via cosine similarity between latent vectors, aligning the geometry of the space with intuitive notions of structural and functional relatedness. Compared to standard Euclidean latent spaces, which are unbounded and lack a fixed notion of scale, the hypersphere offers a bounded and interpretable geometry. The maximum possible distance between any two points is finite, and similarity is always measured with respect to angular separation. In Euclidean space, determining whether one molecule is ``closer” to another depends on arbitrary norms and is always relative to other distances, which can obscure interpretation. On the hypersphere, by contrast, closeness is inherently normalized, and all points lie on the same manifold, making latent relationships both geometrically and chemically more meaningful.

We argue that this spherical constraint provides not only a bounded and regular topology, mitigating issues of drift or unbounded extrapolation, but also a semantically rich space for interpolation. The geodesics on the sphere enable chemically meaningful transitions between molecules, especially relevant in drug discovery and material design, where interpolative synthesis of compounds is often desirable.

