To probe the generative capacity of the learned spherical space, we conducted interpolation experiments using spherical linear interpolation (SLERP) between pairs of latent direction vectors \(\mu_A, \mu_B \in S^{127}\). We generated 11 equidistant points along each geodesic path and decoded them into candidate molecules, assessing chemical validity using RDKit~\citep{RDKit}. By varying the maximum cosine similarity between selected molecular pairs, we evaluated the model’s ability to bridge structurally diverse compounds. More specifically, the latent representations (\(\mu\) vectors) reside on the unit hypersphere \(S^{d-1}\). This allows for a well-defined maximum distance between any two points and enables the use of cosine similarity (the dot product between unit vectors) as an intuitive measure of their relationship. Cosine similarity values range from -1 (diametrically opposed, maximally dissimilar directions) to 1 (identical directions), with 0 indicating orthogonality. A positive dot product suggests that the latent representations point into the same generalized half-space on the sphere, while a negative value indicates opposing orientations, providing a clear framework for interpreting the distance and relatedness of encoded molecules.

The results in Table~\ref{tab:interpolation_validity} demonstrate that even when interpolating between directions facing to opposite half-spaces (cosine similarity \(\leq 0.0\)), the SpCauchy-VAE maintains a high rate of valid molecule generation, exceeding 60\%. These findings underscore the smoothness and chemical coherence of the learned latent space and illustrate the model's promise for molecular discovery tasks, where navigating between structurally distinct compounds is essential. The spherical Cauchy geometry not only supports stable training in high dimensions but also provides a principled framework for semantic interpolation and controlled generation in chemical domains.


\section{Conclusion}

In this work, we have introduced the spCauchy-VAE, a novel variational autoencoder that employs the spherical Cauchy distribution as its latent prior. By naturally constraining the latent space to the unit sphere, our approach captures the inherent directional and cyclic structure present in many real-world datasets. This hyperspherical latent space is not only conceptually appealing but has also been shown, through our theoretical derivations and empirical results, to yield more natural and interpretable representations.

A key advantage of our method is its numerical robustness. Unlike the widely used von Mises-Fisher (vMF) distribution, which relies on modified Bessel functions and can suffer from underflow or overflow issues in certain regimes, the spCauchy distribution circumvents these challenges. By reparameterizing the concentration parameter and expressing the KL divergence in terms of a Gauss hypergeometric function with an argument in \((0,1)\), we ensure rapid convergence and stable numerical evaluation even for large dimensions.

Furthermore, the reparameterization trick is elegantly achieved via a Möbius transformation. Starting with uniform samples on the sphere, the Möbius mapping produces samples from the spCauchy distribution in a fully differentiable manner, allowing efficient gradient-based optimization throughout the VAE.

Our experiments on MNIST confirmed the practical effectiveness of the proposed model. Despite the theoretical disadvantages imposed by the uniform prior and fewer latent parameters, the spCauchy-VAE consistently outperformed the Gaussian baseline in terms of reconstruction accuracy across a range of latent dimensions. The model produced smooth interpolations, organized latent codes meaningfully across the sphere, and converged reliably without requiring special initialization or KL annealing. These results demonstrate that the spCauchy distribution not only offers theoretical elegance but also practical performance advantages in real-world tasks.

To further evaluate the scalability and domain applicability of the spCauchy-VAE, we extended our analysis to the task of molecular generation using SMILES string representations. In this significantly more complex setting, the model successfully learned meaningful embeddings in a 128-dimensional spherical latent space, achieving high reconstruction accuracy and generating chemically valid molecules with strong generalization to unseen data. Importantly, spherical interpolations between latent codes yielded smooth geodesic transitions across molecular structures, with a substantial proportion of valid intermediates even when interpolating between highly dissimilar molecules. 

Looking ahead, an exciting direction for future research is the integration of spherical and Euclidean latent spaces within a single VAE architecture. By concatenating latent codes from both spaces, e.g., a vector from \( \mathbb{S}^d \) and another from \( \mathbb{R}^k \), the model could separately encode cyclic or directional features and unconstrained Euclidean structure, then combine this information in the decoder. Such a hybrid latent space would allow the VAE to leverage the geometric advantages of hyperspherical modeling where appropriate, while retaining the flexibility of standard Gaussian representations. This approach draws parallels with multi-head representation learning, modular latent models, and recent advances in structured latent variable architectures, offering a promising bridge between geometric and probabilistic modeling.

Overall, the spCauchy-VAE provides a compelling alternative to traditional latent distributions. It enables a more faithful hyperspherical characterization of latent spaces, simplifies the computation of the KL divergence, and improves numerical stability, all of which are validated by our experimental results. Future work will further explore its applications and potential advantages across a variety of domains. A particularly promising direction is its application to natural language processing, as it is increasingly evident that embeddings from large language models benefit from being represented on a spherical manifold by being by default normalized. The spCauchy-VAE offers a principled and efficient generative framework for modeling these crucial hyperspherical representations.

