\section{Model}
\label{sec:model}

We consider a balanced market with $n$ agents (set $\mathcal{X}$) and $n$ items (set $\mathcal{Y}$). Each agent $x \in \mathcal{X}$ has a unique priority score $s_x \in \mathbb{R}$. The utility of agent $x$ for item $y$ is given as 
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:utility-definition}
    u_{xy} &= (1-\rho)\,q_y + \rho\,\varphi_{xy}, \ \ \forall x \in \mathcal{X}, y \in \mathcal{Y}
\end{align}
where, $q_y$ and $\varphi_{xy}$ are independent terms and,
\begin{itemize}
    \item $q_y$ is a common term which depends only on item $y$, drawn i.i.d from a distribution $P_q$.
    \item $\varphi_{xy}$ is an idiosyncratic term for the agent-item $(x,y)$ pair, drawn i.i.d from a distribution $P_\varphi$.
    \item $\rho \in [0,1]$ is a parameter that determines the relative weight of the idiosyncratic term, capturing level of heterogeneity. Smaller $\rho$ implies more homogeneous preferences (common term dominates), while larger $\rho$ implies more heterogeneous preferences (idiosyncratic term dominates).
\end{itemize}

Agents select items sequentially in $n$ rounds, ordered by their priority scores (highest score chooses first, etc.). In round $k$, the $k$-th agent chooses from the remaining items (denoted as $\mathcal{Y}_{k}^{\text{rem}}$) to maximize her perceived utility, with ties broken uniformly at random\footnote{This model encompasses the main examples of interest. In the uncapacitated setting, the sequence does not matter because items have infinite capacity. In the capacitated case, a priority-based order aligns with centralized college admissions, where students are ranked by an exam score and sequentially pick from available programs \citep{baswana2019centralized, gale1962college, abdulkadirouglu1998random}: in the balanced market setting with common preferences on the supply side, deferred acceptance is equivalent to serial dictatorship. If priority scores are random, this corresponds to the random arrival model in online marketplaces.}. We study three information regimes as mentioned below. Let $\sigma_{\star}(k)$ be the index of the item chosen by the $k$-th agent in regime $\star \in \{\emptyset, q, u\}$.

\begin{enumerate}[label = $(\roman*)$]
    \item {\sf No Information} ($\emptyset$): The agent has no information about any items, perceives all items as identical, and hence chooses uniformly at random among the remaining items.
    \item {\sf Only Quality Information} ($q$): The agent only knows the common term $(q_y)$ and agent $k$ chooses the item with the highest value of the common term, since the idiosyncratic term for all the items is the same from the agent's point of view. In particular, we have that 
        \begin{align*}
            \sigma_{q}(k) \triangleq  \argmax_{y \in \mathcal{Y}_k^{\text{rem}}} \ \  (1 -\rho) q_y + \rho \varphi_{ky} = \argmax_{y \in \mathcal{Y}_{k}^{\text{rem}}} \ \ q_y, 
        \end{align*}
    where $\varphi_{ky} = 0, \forall y \in \mathcal{Y}_{k}^{\text{rem}}$ since the agents have no information about the idiosyncratic term.
    \item {\sf Full Information} ($u$): The agent knows both the common terms $(q_y)$ as well as the idiosyncratic terms $(\varphi_{xy})$ and agent $k$ chooses the item with the highest utility. In particular, we have that 
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma_u(k) \triangleq \argmax_{y \in \mathcal{Y}^{\text{rem}}_k} \ \ (1 - \rho) q_y + \rho \varphi_{ky}.
    \end{align*}
\end{enumerate}

 We study two types of supply constraints:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
    \item \underline{Uncapacitated Supply}: Each items has \textit{infinite capacity}; any number of agents can choose the same item.
    \item \underline{Capacitated Supply}: Each item has \textit{unit capacity}; once chosen, it becomes unavailable to subsequent agents.
\end{enumerate}
We define \textit{agent welfare} as the (expected) average utility of agents under each regime.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \underline{Agent Welfare in uncapacitated setting}: $\mathsf{AW}_{\star}^{\mathsf{uncap}}(n) 
    \triangleq \mathbb{E}\bigl[u_{1,\sigma_{\star}(1)}\bigr],
    \quad \star \in \{\emptyset,q,u\},$ since all agents effectively face an identical choice as item capacity is infinite.

    \item \underline{Agent Welfare in capacitated setting}: 
    $\mathsf{AW}_{\star}^{\mathsf{cap}}(n) 
    \triangleq n^{-1}\,\mathbb{E} \Bigl[\sum_{k=1}^n u_{k,\sigma_{\star}(k)}\Bigr],
    \quad \star \in \{\emptyset,q,u\}.$

\end{itemize}

To assess the \textit{marginal value} of public rankings and personalized recommendations, we compare welfare across regimes. For the uncapacitated setting, we have that,
\[
\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\mathsf{uncap}}(n) 
\;=\; \mathsf{AW}_{q}^{\mathsf{uncap}}(n)\;-\;\mathsf{AW}_{\emptyset}^{\mathsf{uncap}}(n),
\quad
\Delta_{q \to u}^{\mathsf{uncap}}(n) 
\;=\; \mathsf{AW}_{u}^{\mathsf{uncap}}(n)\;-\;\mathsf{AW}_{q}^{\mathsf{uncap}}(n).
\]
$\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ and $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ quantify the marginal impact of public rankings and personalized recommendations in the uncapacitated setting, respectively. Similarly, for the capacitated setting, we have that
\[
\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\mathsf{cap}}(n) 
\;=\; \mathsf{AW}_{q}^{\mathsf{cap}}(n)\;-\;\mathsf{AW}_{\emptyset}^{\mathsf{cap}}(n),
\quad
\Delta_{q \to u}^{\mathsf{cap}}(n) 
\;=\; \mathsf{AW}_{u}^{\mathsf{cap}}(n)\;-\;\mathsf{AW}_{q}^{\mathsf{cap}}(n).
\]
We have that $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n)$ and $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n)$ quantify the marginal impact of public rankings and personalized recommendations in the capacitated setting, respectively.


