\subsection{Capacitated supply setting}
\label{subsec:welfare-implications-capacitated}

Recall that in the capacitated supply setting, we have $n$ agents and $n$ items where each agent has a unit demand and each item has a unit capacity and there is one-to-one match between agents and items.

\begin{theorem}[Capacitated Supply, Pareto tails]
    \label{thm:capacitated-pareto}
    Consider the capacitated supply setting. Assume that the common terms $(q_y)$ are drawn i.i.d from distribution $P_{q}$ with non-negative support and finite mean $\mu_{q} < \infty$. Fix $c_\varphi > 0$ and $\alpha_{\varphi} > 1$. Assume that the idiosyncratic terms $(\varphi_{xy})$ are drawn i.i.d from distribution $P_{\varphi}$ with non-negative support, finite mean $\mu_{\varphi} < \infty$ and has a Pareto tail with parameters $(c_{\varphi}, \alpha_{\varphi})$. For any $\rho \in [0,1]$, we have that
    \begin{enumerate}[label = $(\thetheorem.\alph*)$]
        \item \label{thm:capacitated-pareto-a} The difference in the agent welfare $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n)$ obtained in the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime and the {\sf No Information} regime is zero, i.e., $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n) = 0$.

        \item \label{thm:capacitated-pareto-b} The difference in the agent welfare $\Delta_{q \to \varphi}^{\sf cap}(n)$ obtained in the {\sf Full Information} regime and the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime increases in the number of items $n$. Define $\mathcal{C}_\varphi \triangleq c_{\varphi} (\alpha_\varphi / (\alpha_{\varphi} + 1)) \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_{\varphi})$. Then we have that,
        \begin{align*}
            \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Delta^{\sf cap}_{q \to u}(n)}{ \mathcal{C}_\varphi \cdot n^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}}} = \rho.
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

The proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto} is deferred to Section \ref{sec:proof-capacitated-supply-setting}. Theorem~\ref{thm:capacitated-pareto} analyzes how public rankings and personalized recommendations affect welfare in a capacity-constrained setting.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a} studies whether \textsf{Only Quality Information} (public rankings) improves welfare over \textsf{No Information}.
    \item Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b} studies the additional benefit from \textsf{Only Quality Information} (public rankings) to \textsf{Full Information} (personalized recommendations).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{\underline{Discussion of Theorem~\ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a} (Value of Public Rankings)}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Main Insights}:
    Even if the common terms $q_y$ follow \textit{any} distribution (not necessarily Pareto or exponential), public rankings do \textit{not} increase the total welfare under unit-capacity constraints (see Figure \ref{subfig:capacitated-pareto-tail-a}). The key reason is that the \textit{total common value} across items is limited by capacity constraints, so revealing $q_y$ merely reshuffles who claims which item but does not increase the aggregate utility. 
    Moreover, this conclusion remains valid in a more general setting where items can have capacities $C_y>0$ (see Remark~\ref{rmk:capacitated-case-extension}).

    \item \textbf{Proof Sketch}: 
    In \textsf{Only Quality Information} regime, each agent bases their choice on $q_y$, but the idiosyncratic component $\varphi_{xy}$ is an independent random draw. 
    Since every agent effectively gets a “fresh” idiosyncratic draw for whichever item they pick, the expected total utility matches that in \textsf{No Information}. 
    This argument requires $(i)$ independence between $q_y$ and $\varphi_{xy}$ and $(ii)$ a bounded sum of $q_y$’s. 
    See Section~\ref{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-a} for details.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{\underline{Discussion of Theorem~\ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b} (Value of Personalized Recommendations)}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Main Insights}:
    In the capacitated setting, all the value lies in personalized recommendations.
    Allowing agents to see both the common and idiosyncratic terms (\textsf{Full Information}) generates substantial welfare gains (see Figure \ref{subfig:capacitated-pareto-tail-b}). We show that $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n) \asymp \rho \cdot C_\varphi \cdot n^{1 / \alpha_q}$, where $C_\varphi$ is a constant which depends on $c_\varphi$ and $\alpha_\varphi$. Revealing $\varphi_{xy}$ matches each agent to an item that offers higher individual utility—significantly boosting total welfare. Our result also illuminates the role of level of heterogeneity. The welfare gain due to personalization of recommendations scale linearly in $\rho$: larger the value of $\rho$, larger the heterogeneity in preferences and larger the impact of personalized recommendations.

    \item \textbf{Proof Sketch}:
    By deferred decisions \citep{mitzenmacher2017probability}, we can imagine that when agent $k$ arrives, the $n-k$ relevant idiosyncratic values are drawn afresh. Thus, agent $k$’s final utility is at most $(1-\rho)\,q_{\sigma_u(k)} + \rho\,\varphi_{(n-k:n-k)}$ and at least $\rho\,\varphi_{(n-k:n-k)}$. Summing over all agents yields a total gain on the order of $\rho\,n^{1/\alpha_\varphi}$, because the maximum of $(n-k)$ Pareto draws scales like $(n-k)^{1/\alpha_\varphi}$. We formalize this argument in Lemma~\ref{lem:upper-lower-bound-capacitated-quality-full-info-diff} and Proposition~\ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}. See Section~\ref{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-b} for the complete proof.
\end{itemize}

% \paragraph{Discussion of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a}.} To quantify the impact of public rankings on agent welfare, we first consider the difference in agent welfare obtained in the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime and the agent welfare obtained in the {\sf No Information} regime, denoted as $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n)$. Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a} shows that this difference $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n)$ is zero, which is verified via simulations in Figure \ref{subfig:capacitated-pareto-tail-a}. This implies that while rankings help agents refine their estimates of the utility by helping them learn about the common term, at an aggregate level, they provide no value since the total common value is limited by capacity constraints. In the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime, the agents base their decisions solely on the common term and the idiosyncratic term associated with their chosen item is some random draw from the $P_{\varphi}$ distribution, therefore the expected total agent welfare is the same as when the agents chose the items randomly. This intuition is formalized into a proof in Section \ref{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-a}. Note that the assumption that the idiosyncratic term is independent of the common term is crucial for the result in Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a} to hold. Furthermore, the result in Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a}  holds for arbitrary values of the common terms, provided their sum is bounded and only requires the idiosyncratic terms to be independent of the common terms and have a finite mean.

% \paragraph{Discussion of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}.} To quantify the impact of personalized recommendations which help agents learn their idiosyncratic component of the utility in addition to the public rankings which inform agents of the common component, we consider the difference in the agent welfare obtained in the {\sf Full Information} regime and the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime, denoted as $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n)$. Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b} shows that this difference $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n)$ scales as $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n) \asymp C_{\varphi} (1 - \rho)n^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}}$. We verify this numerically via simulations as shown in Figure \ref{subfig:capacitated-pareto-tail-b}. Note that the characterization in Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b} only depends on the tail behaviour of the distribution of the idiosyncratic term $P_\varphi$. This result implies that significant boost in agent welfare can be realized by providing agents with personalized recommendations which helps them learn both the components of their utility. 

% {\it Proof Sketch and Intuition of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}.} % We will provide a brief sketch of the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b} to convey the intuition and driver of the result.
% Let $\varphi_{k,(n - k: n- k)}$ denote the maximum of $n - k$ values drawn from $P_\varphi$. Now, since the idiosyncratic terms are drawn independent of the common term, using the principle of deferred decisions \citep{mitzenmacher2017probability}, we can consider an equivalent setting where the idiosyncratic term corresponding to an agent-item pair is drawn when it is the agents turn to choose the item. Therefore, when the agent $k$ chooses amongst the $n - k$ items, the $n - k$ idiosyncratic terms are drawn i.i.d from distribution $P_\varphi$. Let $\sigma_u(k)$ denote the choice of agent $k$ and let $u_{\sigma_{u}(k)}$ denote the utility the agent $k$ derives by choosing item $\sigma_u(k)$. Now we have that $u_{\sigma_{u}(k)}$ is bounded above by $(1 - \rho) q_{\sigma_{u}(k)} + \rho \varphi_{k,(n - k: n - k)}$, i.e., the idiosyncratic component of the item chosen by the agent $k$ is at most the highest idiosyncratic term amongst $n - k$ draws from $P_\varphi$. Moreover, the utility of agent $k$, $u_{\sigma_{u}(k)}$ must be at least $\rho \varphi_{k, (n - k : n - k)}$ because the agent can always choose the item with the highest idiosyncratic term value (ignoring the common term value) and will derived at least a utility of $\rho \varphi_{k, (n - k : n - k)}$. Combining these two results in a lower and upper bound on the total agent welfare in the {\sf Full Information} regime which is formalized in Lemma \ref{lem:upper-lower-bound-capacitated-quality-full-info-diff}. Combined with the fact that expected value of maximum of $n$ i.i.d random variables with Pareto tails with parameters $(c, \alpha)$ scales as $C \cdot n^{1  / \alpha}$, where $C$ is a constant, (formalized in Proposition \ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}), we get the result in Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}. The aforementioned sketch is formalized in Section \ref{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-b}.

