\begin{theorem}[Capacitated Supply, Exponential tails]
    \label{thm:capacitated-exponential}
    Consider the capacitated supply setting. Assume that common terms $(q_y)$ are drawn i.i.d from distribution $P_{q}$ with non-negative support and finite mean $\mu_{q} < \infty$. Fix $c_\varphi > 0, \lambda_{\varphi} >0$. Assume that the idiosyncratic terms $(\varphi_{xy})$ are drawn i.i.d from distribution $P_{\varphi}$ with non-negative support, finite mean $\mu_{\varphi} < \infty$ and has an exponential tail with parameters $(c_\varphi, \lambda_\varphi)$. For any $\rho \in [0,1]$, we have that
    \begin{enumerate}[label = $(\thetheorem.\alph*)$]
        \item \label{thm:capacitated-exponential-a} The difference in the agent welfare $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n)$ obtained in the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime and the {\sf No Information} regime is zero, i.e., $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n) = 0$.

        \item \label{thm:capacitated-exponential-b} The difference in the agent welfare $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n)$ obtained in the {\sf Full Information} regime and the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime increases in the number of items $n$. In particular, we have that
        \begin{align*}
            \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Delta^{\sf cap}_{ q \to u}(n)}{\ln n / \lambda_{\varphi}} = \rho.
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

We prove Theorem~\ref{thm:capacitated-exponential} in Appendix~\ref{app:proof-thm-capacitated-exponential}. 
The only essential difference from Theorem~\ref{thm:capacitated-pareto} is the \textit{scaling} of $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n)$, 
which here grows as $\rho \,\ln(n)/\lambda_{\varphi}$ (rather than $n^{1/\alpha_{\varphi}}$). 
This follows from the fact that the maximum of $n$ i.i.d.\ exponential($\lambda$) random variables scales on the order of $\ln(n)/\lambda$ 
(see Proposition~\ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-exponential-tail}). 
Figures~\ref{subfig:capacitated-exponential-tail-a} and~\ref{subfig:capacitated-exponential-tail-b} illustrate 
Theorem~\ref{thm:capacitated-exponential-a} and \ref{thm:capacitated-exponential-b} via numerical simulations, 
assuming an exponential distribution with rate $\lambda=1$ for the idiosyncratic terms.


