\section{Proof of Theorems for utility distributions with Pareto tail}
\label{sec:proofs}
%In this section, we will provide the proof of Theorems \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto}, \ref{thm:capacitated-exponential}, \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto} and \ref{thm:uncapacitated-exponential}.

In this section, we provide the proof of our results for distributions with Pareto tails, i.e., Theorem \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto} for the uncapacitated supply setting and Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto} for the capacitated supply setting. The case of distribution with exponential tails shares common ideas to that of distributions with Pareto tails and hence has been deferred to Appendix \ref{app:proof-exponential}.


\subsection{Useful Results}
We will first state a useful proposition which will be used in proving Theorems \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto} and \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}.

\begin{proposition}
    \label{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}
    Fix $c > 0$ and $\alpha > 1$. Let $X$ be a random variable with distribution $P$. Assume that $X \geq 0$ and $\mathbb{E}[X] < \infty$. 
    Assume that the distribution $P$ has a Pareto tail with parameters $(c, \alpha)$. Let $X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n$ be i.i.d copies of $X$ and define $X_{(n:n)} \triangleq \max_{1 \leq k \leq n} X_k$. We have that 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{n \to \infty } \frac{\mathbb{E}[X_{(n:n)}]}{c\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha) \cdot n^{1 / \alpha}} = 1.
    \end{align*}
\end{proposition}
The proof of Proposition \ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail} is deferred to Appendix \ref{app:proof-expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}.



\subsection{Uncapacitated Supply Setting}
\label{subsec:proof-thm-uncapacitated-pareto}
In this section, we will provide the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto}. 

\subsubsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto-a}}
\label{subsec:proof-thm-uncapacitated-pareto-a}
\begin{proof}[\underline{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto-a}}]
In the uncapacitated setting, there is a single agent and $n$ items and in the {\sf No Information} regime, the agent chooses a random item since the agent has no information about the common terms $(q_y)$ or the idiosyncratic terms $(\varphi_{xy})$. Recall that $\sigma_{\emptyset}(1)$ denotes the index of the item chosen by the agent.
Therefore, the social welfare ${\sf AW}_{\emptyset}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ is given as 
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:sw-no-info-uncap-pareto}
    {\sf AW}_{\emptyset}^{\sf uncap}(n) = \mathbb{E}\left[ (1 - \rho) q_{\sigma_{\emptyset}(1)} + \rho \varphi_{1\sigma_{\emptyset}(1)}\right] = (1 - \rho) \mu_q + \rho \mu_\varphi,
\end{align}
where the last equality follows from the fact that index $\sigma_\emptyset(1)$ is uniformly random in $\{1,2,\dots, n\}$.

In the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime, the agent chooses the item with highest common term value. Therefore, the social welfare ${\sf AW}_{q}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ is given as 
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:sw-quality-info-uncap-pareto}
    {\sf AW}_{q}^{\sf uncap}(n) = \mathbb{E}\left[ ( 1 - \rho) q_{\sigma_{q}(1)} + \rho \varphi_{1 \sigma_{q}(1)} \right] = \mathbb{E}\left[(1 - \rho) q_{(n:n)} + \rho \varphi_{1 \sigma_{q}(1)} \right] = (1 - \rho) \mathbb{E}[q_{(n:n)}] + \rho \mu_{\varphi},
\end{align}
where the last equality follows from the fact that index $\sigma_q(1)$ is uniformly random in $\{1,2,\dots, n\}$. Using \eqref{eq:sw-no-info-uncap-pareto}, \eqref{eq:sw-quality-info-uncap-pareto} and Proposition \ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}, we get the required result.
\end{proof}

\subsubsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto-b}}
\label{subsec:proof-thm-uncapacitated-pareto-b}

We will begin by stating an important result in the form of Lemma \ref{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails} which will be crucial in proving Theorem \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto-b}.

\begin{lemma}
    \label{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails}
    Fix $\rho \in (0,1)$. Fix $c_X > 0, \alpha_X > 1, c_Y > 0, \alpha_Y > 1$. Let $X$ be a random variable with non-negative support, finite mean $\mu_X < \infty$ and has a Pareto tail with parameters $(c_X, \alpha_X)$. Let $Y$ be another random variable with non-negative support, finite mean $\mu_Y < \infty$ and has a Pareto tail with parameters $(c_Y, \alpha_Y)$. Define $Z = (1 - \rho) X + \rho Y$. Then we have that $Z \geq 0$, $\mathbb{E}[Z] = \mu_Z = (1 - \rho) \mu_X + \rho \mu_Y < \infty$ and has a Pareto tail with parameters $(c_Z, \alpha_Z)$ given as 
    \begin{enumerate}[label = $(\thelemma.\alph*)$]
        \item \label{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails-a} \underline{$\alpha_X < \alpha_Y$.} $c_Z = ( 1 - \rho) c_X$ and $\alpha_Z = \alpha_X$.
        \item \label{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails-b} \underline{$\alpha_X > \alpha_Y$.} $c_Z = \rho c_Y$ and $\alpha_Z = \alpha_Y$.
        \item \label{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails-c} \underline{$\alpha_X = \alpha_Y : = \alpha$.} $c_Z = ((( 1 - \rho) c_X)^\alpha + (\rho c_Y)^{\alpha})^{1 / \alpha}$ and $\alpha_Z = \alpha$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

Lemma \ref{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails} follows from \citep[Lemma 2.18]{nair2022fundamentals} and for completeness we provide a proof in Appendix \ref{app:proof-tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails}.


\begin{proof}[\underline{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto-b}}]
Since there is only one agent, we will denote $\varphi_{1k} = \varphi_{k}$ for all $k \in \{1,2,\dots, n\}$. We will begin by proving part \ref{thm:uncapacitated-pareto-b-i}. Define $Z_k = (1 - \rho) q_k + \rho \varphi_{k}$. In the {\sf Full Information} regime, the agent will choose the item with the value $\max\{Z_1, Z_2,\dots, Z_n\}$. Therefore, the social welfare ${\sf AW}_{u}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ is given as
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:sw-full-info-uncap-pareto}
    {\sf AW}_u^{\sf uncap}(n) = \mathbb{E}\left[\max_{1 \leq k \leq n} (1 - \rho) q_k + \rho \varphi_k \right] = \mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}].
\end{align}
Next we consider the different cases:
\begin{enumerate}[label = $(\roman*)$]
    \item $\alpha_q \neq \alpha_\varphi$. Assume that $\alpha_q < \alpha_\varphi$, then we have that 
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\Delta^{\sf uncap}_{q \to u}(n)}{c_q\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_q) n^{1 / \alpha_q}} &\stackrel{(a)}= \frac{\mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}]}{c_q\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_q) n^{1 / \alpha_q}} - \frac{(1 - \rho) \mathbb{E}[q_{(n
        :n)}]}{c_q\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_q) n^{1 / \alpha_q}} -  \frac{\rho \mu_\varphi}{c_q\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_q) n^{1 / \alpha_q}}, \\
        &\stackrel{(b)}= \frac{(1 - \rho) \mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}]}{c_Z\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_Z) n^{1 / \alpha_Z}} - \frac{(1 - \rho) \mathbb{E}[q_{(n
        :n)}]}{c_q\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_q) n^{1 / \alpha_q}} -  \frac{\rho \mu_\varphi}{c_q\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_q) n^{1 / \alpha_q}},
    \end{align*}
    where $(a)$ follows from the fact that $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n) = {\sf AW}_{u}^{\sf uncap}(n) - {\sf AW}_{q}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ and \eqref{eq:sw-quality-info-uncap-pareto} and \eqref{eq:sw-full-info-uncap-pareto}, $(b)$ follows from Lemma \ref{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails} for $\alpha_q < \alpha_\varphi$. Using Proposition \ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}, we have that
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}]}{c_Z \Gamma(1 - 1/ \alpha_Z) n^{1 / \alpha_Z}} = 1, \ \ \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\mathbb{E}[q_{(n:n)}]}{c_q \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_q)n^{1 / \alpha_q}} = 1,
    \end{align*}
    which in turn implies that $\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n)}{c_q \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_q) n^{1 / \alpha_{q}}} = 0$. 

    Next we assume that $\alpha_q > \alpha_{\varphi}$, then we have that
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n)}{c_\varphi \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}} &\stackrel{(a)}= \frac{\mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}]}{c_\varphi\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}} - \frac{(1 - \rho) \mathbb{E}[q_{(n
        :n)}]}{c_\varphi\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}} -  \frac{\rho \mu_\varphi}{c_\varphi\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}}, \\
        &\stackrel{(b)}= \frac{\rho \mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}]}{c_Z\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_Z) n^{1 / \alpha_Z}} - \frac{(1 - \rho) \mathbb{E}[q_{(n
        :n)}]}{c_\varphi\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}} -  \frac{\rho \mu_\varphi}{c_\varphi\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}},
    \end{align*}
    where $(a)$ follows from the fact that $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n) = {\sf AW}_{u}^{\sf uncap}(n) - {\sf AW}_{q}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ and \eqref{eq:sw-quality-info-uncap-pareto} and \eqref{eq:sw-full-info-uncap-pareto}, $(b)$ follows from Lemma \ref{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails} for $\alpha_q > \alpha_\varphi$. Using Proposition \ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}, since $\alpha_q > \alpha_{\varphi}$, we have that 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\mathbb{E}[q_{(n:n)}]}{c_\varphi \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}} = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\mathbb{E}[q_{(n:n)}]}{c_\varphi \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_q}} \cdot \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{n^{1 / \alpha_q}}{n^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}}} = 0,
    \end{align*}
    which in turn implies that $\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n)}{c_\varphi \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_\varphi) n^{1 / \alpha_\varphi}} = \rho$. 

    \item $\alpha_q = \alpha_\varphi$. Denote $\alpha := \alpha_q = \alpha_\varphi$. Then we have that,
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\Delta^{\sf uncap}_{q \to u}(n)}{\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha) n^{1 / \alpha}} &\stackrel{(a)}= \frac{\mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}]}{\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha) n^{1 / \alpha}} - \frac{(1 - \rho) \mathbb{E}[q_{(n:n)}]}{\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha) n^{1 / \alpha}} -  \frac{\rho \mu_\varphi}{\Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha) n^{1 / \alpha}}, 
    \end{align*}
    where $(a)$ follows from the fact that $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n) = {\sf AW}_{u}^{\sf uncap}(n) - {\sf AW}_{q}^{\sf uncap}(n)$ and \eqref{eq:sw-quality-info-uncap-pareto} and \eqref{eq:sw-full-info-uncap-pareto}. Using Lemma \ref{lem:tail-convex-combination-pareto-tails} and Proposition \ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail}, we have that 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\mathbb{E}[Z_{(n:n)}]}{\Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha) n^{1 / \alpha}} = (((1 - \rho) c_q)^\alpha + (\rho c_{\varphi})^{\alpha})^{1 / \alpha}, \ \ \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\mathbb{E}[q_{(n:n)}]}{\Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha) n^{1 / \alpha}} = (1 - \rho) c_q,
    \end{align*}
    which in turn implies that $\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf uncap}(n)}{\Gamma(1 - 1/ \alpha) n^{1 / \alpha}} = (((1 - \rho) c_q)^\alpha + (\rho c_{\varphi})^{\alpha})^{1 / \alpha} - (1 - \rho) c_q$. The case of $c_q = c_\varphi = c$ follows trivially.
\end{enumerate}
    This completes the proof.
\end{proof}



\subsection{Capacitated supply setting}
\label{sec:proof-capacitated-supply-setting}
In this section, we will provide the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto}. Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto} has two parts: $(a)$ characterizes the difference between the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime and {\sf No Information} regime $\Delta_{\varphi \to q}^{\sf cap}(n)$ and $(b)$ characterizes the difference between the {\sf Full Information} regime and the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime $\Delta_{q \to u}^{\sf cap}(n)$. %Note that the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a} and \ref{thm:capacitated-exponential-a} is common and hence will be presented as such in Section \ref{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-a}. 
%The proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b} will make use of Proposition \ref{prop:expected-maximum-n-pareto-tail} and the key Lemma \ref{lem:upper-lower-bound-capacitated-quality-full-info-diff}. We provide these useful intermediate results in Section \ref{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-b}.

\subsubsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a} }
\label{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-a}
\begin{proof}[\underline{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-a}}]
In the {\sf No Information} regime, since the agents do not have information about the common term or the idiosyncratic term, they randomly choose an item from the remaining set of items. Recall that $\sigma_\emptyset(k)$ denotes the index of the item chosen by agent $k$ in the {\sf No Information} regime. Therefore we have that,
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:sw-cap-no-info}
    %\nonumber
    {\sf AW}^{\sf cap}_{\emptyset}(n) %&\stackrel{(a)}= \frac{1}{n} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{k = 1}^n u_{k\sigma_{\emptyset}(k)} \right] %\\
    %\nonumber
    &\stackrel{(a)}= \frac{1}{n}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{k = 1}^n (1 - \rho) q_{\sigma_{\emptyset}(k)} + \rho \varphi_{k \sigma_{\emptyset}(k)} \right]
    %&
    \stackrel{(b)}= (1 - \rho) \frac{1}{n} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k = 1}^n q_{k} \right] + \rho \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k = 1}^n \mathbb{E}[\varphi_{k \sigma_{\emptyset}(k)}],  
\end{align}
where $(a)$ follows from definition of $u_{k\sigma_{\empty}(k)}$, $(b)$ follows from the fact that $\sum_{k = 1}^n q_{\sigma_{\emptyset}(k)} = \sum_{k = 1}^n q_k$.

In the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime, the agents base their decisions solely on the common term $(q_y)$. Therefore, we have that the agent $k$ will choose the item with common term value $q_{(k:n)}$ (recall that $X_{(k:n)}$ denotes the $k$-th smallest value of $n$ i.i.d copies of $X$). 
Recall that $\sigma_{q}(k)$ denotes the index of the item chosen by agent $k$ in the {\sf Only Quality Information} regime. This means that $q_{\sigma_q(k)} = q_{(k:n)}$. Therefore we have that,
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:sw-cap-quality-info}
    {\sf AW}_{q}^{\sf cap}(n) &\stackrel{(a)}= \frac{1}{n}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{k = 1}^n (1 - \rho) q_{\sigma_{q}(k)} + \rho \varphi_{k \sigma_{q}(k)} \right]
    %&
    \stackrel{(b)}= (1 - \rho) \frac{1}{n} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k = 1}^n q_{k} \right] + \rho \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k = 1}^n \mathbb{E}[\varphi_{k \sigma_{q}(k)}], 
\end{align}
where $(a)$ follows from definition of $u_{k\sigma_{q}(k)}$, $(b)$ follows from the fact that $\sum_{k = 1}^n q_{\sigma_{\emptyset}(k)} = \sum_{k = 1}^n q_{(k:n)} = \sum_{k = 1}^n q_k$.
Note that the index $\sigma_{\emptyset}(k)$ and $\sigma_{q}(k)$ are random and hence we have that $\varphi_{k \sigma_{\emptyset}(k)} \stackrel{d}= \varphi_{k \sigma_q(k)}$ (have the same distribution) and therefore $\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{k \sigma_{\emptyset}(k)}] = \mathbb{E}[\varphi_{k \sigma_q(k)}]$. Comparing \eqref{eq:sw-cap-no-info} and \eqref{eq:sw-cap-quality-info}, we have that $\Delta_{\emptyset \to q}^{\sf cap}(n) = 0$.
\end{proof}


\subsubsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}}
\label{subsec:proof-theorem-capacitated-pareto-exponential-b}
We first present a key lemma which will be useful in proving Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}.
\begin{lemma}
    \label{lem:upper-lower-bound-capacitated-quality-full-info-diff}
    Consider the capacitated supply setting.~Assume that the common terms $(q_y)$ are drawn i.i.d from distribution $P_q$ with non-negative support and finite mean $\mu_q < \infty$. Assume that the idiosyncratic terms $(\varphi_{xy})$ are drawn i.i.d from distribution $P_{\varphi}$ with non-negative support and finite mean $\mu_{\varphi}$. Let $\varphi_{k,(n - k: n - k)}$ denote that the maximum value amongst $n - k$ i.i.d draws from distribution $P_{\varphi}$. Define $\Phi_n \triangleq n^{-1} \sum_{k = 1}^n \mathbb{E}[\varphi_{k,(n - k : n - k)}]$. Then for all $\rho \in [0,1]$, we have that
    \begin{align*}
        -\frac{(1 - \rho) \mu_{q} + \rho \mu_{\varphi}}{\Phi_n} + \rho  \leq \frac{\Delta^{\sf cap}_{q \to u}(n)}{\Phi_n} \leq -  \frac{\rho \mu_{\varphi}}{\Phi_n} + \rho .
    \end{align*}
\end{lemma}
We defer the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:upper-lower-bound-capacitated-quality-full-info-diff} to Appendix \ref{app:proof-upper-lower-bound-capacitated-quality-full-info-diff}. In the case of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}, it suffices to show that $ \lim_{n \to \infty} \Phi_n / (\mathcal{C}_\varphi \cdot n^{1 /\alpha_{\varphi}}) = 1$, where $\mathcal{C}_\varphi$ is defined in Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}. % Similarly, in the case of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-exponential-b}, it suffices to show that $ \lim_{n \to \infty} \Phi_n / (\ln n / \lambda) = 1$.

\begin{proof}[\underline{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:capacitated-pareto-b}}]
 Let us denote $\varphi_{k, (n-k : n - k)} : = \varphi_{(n - k , n- k)}$. Fix $\epsilon > 0$. There exists an $k_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ such for all $k \geq k_0$, we have that 
    \begin{align}
        \label{eq:upper-lower-bound-expectation-max-m-pareto}
        (1 - \epsilon) c \Gamma(1 - 1/\alpha_{\varphi}) \cdot k^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}} \leq \mathbb{E}\left[\varphi_{(k:k)} \right] \leq (1 + \epsilon) c \Gamma(1 - 1/ \alpha_{\varphi}) \cdot k^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}}
    \end{align}
    We can upper bound $\Phi_n$ as follows:
    \begin{align}
        \nonumber
        \Phi_n &\stackrel{(a)}= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{k = 1}^n \mathbb{E}[ \varphi_{(k:k)}] \stackrel{(b)}= \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k = 1}^{m_0} \mathbb{E}[\varphi_{(k:k)}] + \frac{1}{n}\sum_{k = m_0}^n \mathbb{E}[\varphi_{(k:k)}], \\
        \nonumber
        &\stackrel{(c)}\leq \mu_{\varphi} \frac{m_0(m_0 + 1)}{2n} + \frac{1}{n} (1 + \epsilon) c \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_{\varphi}) \sum_{k = m_0}^n k^{1/\alpha_\varphi}, \\
        \nonumber
        &\stackrel{(d)}\leq \mu_{\varphi} \frac{m_0(m_0 + 1)}{2n} + \frac{1}{n} (1 + \epsilon) c \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_{\varphi}) \int_0^n x^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}} dx , \\
        \nonumber
        &\stackrel{(e)}= \mu_{\varphi} \frac{m_0(m_0 + 1)}{2n} + (1 + \epsilon) c \frac
        {\alpha_{\varphi}}{1 + \alpha_{\varphi}} \Gamma(1 - 1 / \alpha_{\varphi})n^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}},
    \end{align}
    where $(a)$ follows from the definition of $\Phi_n$, $(b)$ follows trivially, $(c)$ follows from \eqref{eq:upper-lower-bound-expectation-max-m-pareto} and the fact that $\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{(k:k)}] \leq k \mu_{\varphi}$ for all $k \leq m_0$ since $\mathbb{E}[\max\{X_1, X_2, \dots, X_k\}] \leq \mathbb{E}[\sum_{j = 1}^k X_j] = k \mathbb{E}[X]$, $(d)$ follows from the fact that $\sum_{k = m_0}^n k^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}} \leq \int_{0}^n x^{1 /\alpha_{\varphi}} dx$, $(e)$ follows from evaluating the integral. Using this we have that
    \begin{align*}
        \limsup_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Phi_n}{\mathcal{C}_\varphi \cdot n^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}}} \leq 1 + \epsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Using similar arguments as above, we can easily show that 
    \begin{align*}
        \liminf_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Phi_n}{\mathcal{C}_\varphi \cdot n^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}}} \geq 1 - \epsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Since this holds for all $\epsilon > 0$, we have that $\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\Phi_n}{\mathcal{C}_{\varphi} \cdot n^{1 / \alpha_{\varphi}}} = 1$ and this completes the proof.
\end{proof}








\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

In this work, we examine the impact of public rankings and personalized recommendations on agent welfare in different marketplace settings. To isolate and quantify the impact of these information provisioning tools, we study a stylized model where the agents utility for the items comprises of two terms: $(i)$ a common term and $(ii)$ an idiosyncratic term and both these terms are independent of each other. Public rankings enable the agents to learn about the common term whereas personalized recommendations help the agents to learn about their idiosyncratic component about the items. We quantify the agent welfare under different distributional assumptions on the common and the idiosyncratic terms and under different marketplace settings. Our findings reveal a fundamental interplay between the benefits of these information tools and supply-side constraints. Specifically, in supply-constrained settings, public rankings alone offer limited value in enhancing agent welfare. However, personalized recommendations unlock substantial value by refining individual utility estimates and improving the allocation of agents to items, thereby reducing congestion. Conversely, in supply-unconstrained settings, public rankings significantly enhance welfare by identifying the best overall options, while the impact of personalized recommendations becomes more nuanced.
This contrast arises because public rankings primarily serve to highlight the top items in general, while personalized recommendations serve a dual role: $(i)$ they help agents refine their utility assessments beyond what rankings provide, and $(ii)$ they facilitate a more efficient allocation by mitigating congestion. In capacity-constrained environments, both effects of personalized recommendations are crucial, thus unlocking significant value. In environments without capacity constraints, only the first effect is relevant, leading to a situation where both public rankings and personalized recommendations contribute, but in distinct ways, to agent welfare.

This work takes a first step toward a principled understanding of how various information-provisioning tools perform across different marketplace settings. Our model is deliberately stylized to provide crisp insights, yet it opens several avenues for further investigation. A central assumption in our analysis is the independence of the idiosyncratic terms across agent–item pairs, which plays a critical role in driving our results and simplifies significant technical challenges. In reality, these terms may be correlated, and understanding how such correlation affects the impact of different information-provisioning tools is a promising direction for future research. We have focused exclusively on \textit{agent welfare}; extending the analysis to encompass broader objectives, such as \textit{social welfare} (which also accounts for the utility of the supply side), would provide a more comprehensive assessment of these tools. Finally, in the capacitated setting, our main technical challenge has been to provide a precise welfare characterization for personalized recommendations. While we succeed in giving asymptotically tight upper and lower bounds for Pareto and exponential-tailed distributions, those bounds may not be sharp for more general distributions (e.g., with bounded support). Tightening these bounds in more general scenarios remains a challenging open problem for future work.

